 ['C','One difficulty is interference. Wakeful participants encounter new information that may compete with the original material, whereas sleepers are largely protected from it. Another is fatigue: people kept awake may perform poorly because they are tired at the time of testing. A careful experiment therefore needs comparison conditions, not merely a dramatic difference between two groups. The study described here did not examine children.'],
 ['D','For students, the research supports a practical combination of sufficient sleep and spaced revision. Spacing means returning to material on separate occasions rather than repeating it continuously in one sitting. Retrieval practice, such as answering a question without looking at notes, helps reveal what has actually been retained. Rereading can feel fluent even when the learner cannot later produce the information independently.'],
 ['E','None of this establishes a universal bedtime or guarantees a particular examination score. Sleep needs vary, and laboratory word lists differ from complex classroom learning. The strongest advice is therefore to protect a regular sleep schedule while checking understanding actively. In the hypothetical experiment, researchers recorded recall accuracy but did not measure participants’ future grades or their usual caffeine consumption. Claims about either outcome would require additional evidence.']
