 id:'timekeeping',group:'history',name:'Measuring time',icon:'clock',level:'Foundation',title:'When the town agreed on time',subtitle:'A fictional railway town learns to share a clock',
 paragraphs:[
 ['A','Before fast travel linked distant places, local time could be adequate for daily life. A town might set its clock by the sun without worrying that another town used a slightly different time. A journey lasting several days rarely required minute-by-minute coordination. Faster transport made these small differences a practical problem.'],
 ['B','In the fictional town of Eastmere, three public clocks showed different times in 1874. The stationmaster complained that passengers could arrive after a train had departed even though the market clock suggested they were early. A new railway timetable used the capital’s standard time. The town council debated whether its market clock should be adjusted to match.'],
 ['C','Shopkeepers initially objected because customers relied on familiar routines. The council therefore kept both local and standard time visible for a month, using two labelled clock faces at the station. Printed notices explained the difference. After the transition, public clocks were aligned, although the council did not inspect every household watch.'],
 ['D','Agreement did not guarantee accuracy. A clock could lose minutes if poorly maintained, so the railway appointed a technician to check the station clock each week. Signals transmitted by telegraph provided a reference. The system combined a shared convention with an ongoing maintenance practice; adopting the standard once was not enough to keep every clock correct.'],
 ['E','Eastmere’s example distinguishes measuring time from coordinating behaviour. People needed a trustworthy clock, but they also needed to agree which clock should govern departures and appointments. Standardisation simplified travel while changing local habits. The surviving fictional council record describes the transition, but gives no evidence about whether residents felt that their lives had become more hurried.']
 ],
 tf:[['Small local time differences became more troublesome with faster transport.','TRUE','A explains this connection.'],['All three Eastmere clocks agreed in 1874.','FALSE','B says they showed different times.'],['The new railway timetable used the capital’s standard time.','TRUE','B states the reference time.'],['The council checked every household watch.','FALSE','C says it did not.'],['Residents reported feeling more hurried after the change.','NOT GIVEN','E says there is no evidence about this feeling.']],
